From 9733d06abb7151c8413e8c136d4efce4123fb02a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 00:06:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] feat(mcp): follow relations by type and direction in
 build_context

Relations already carry the type their author wrote
(`- caused_by [[Outage]]`) and prose wiki links are indexed as implicit
`links_to`, but the context traversal joined every edge alike. With
`max_related` capping the result set and the cut made by `ORDER BY depth,
type, id`, mentions and typed relations competed for the same budget and
which ones survived was arbitrary.

This adds three optional traversal filters, threaded from the MCP tool
through the v2 API and MemoryClient down to ContextService.find_related:

- `relation_types`: follow only edges whose type is in the list
- `exclude_relation_types`: skip edges whose type is in the list
  (`["links_to"]` keeps just the explicitly typed graph)
- `direction`: `outgoing` (what the note declares), `incoming` (what
  points at it), or `both` (default, unchanged behaviour)

Filters apply at every hop of the recursive CTE, so
`relation_types=["depends_on"]` at depth 2 returns transitive dependencies
and nothing else. The primary lookup and pagination are untouched; with
no filter the query is byte-identical to before. Type labels are bound
as expanding parameters and match exactly as authored.

`bm tool build-context` gains `--relation-type`, `--exclude-relation-type`
(both repeatable) and `--direction`. The MCP tool also normalizes comma
strings in the function body, because plain-function callers bypass the
schema's BeforeValidator and a bare str would otherwise be sent as one
query parameter per character.

Regenerates the build-context(3) SYNOPSIS and documents the filters in
the man page, the extended assistant guide and the CHANGELOG.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
